    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`BUILD SUCCESS`, tests green (0 failures/errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nI agree with the finding and fixed it. The disposable-provider list was genuinely maintained as two disconnected literals \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS` and `OwnerMapper.DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_BASE_LABELS` \u2014 with the mapper's Javadoc *asserting* a relationship that nothing in code enforced. That's exactly the divergence `E164PhoneNumbers` and `IdentityKeyUtils` exist to prevent.\n\n**What I changed:**\n\n1. **New `util/DisposableEmailDomains.java`** \u2014 a shared single source of truth, styled after `E164PhoneNumbers`:\n   - `BLOCKLIST` \u2014 the authoritative disposable domains.\n   - `BASE_LABELS` \u2014 *derived* from `BLOCKLIST` (each domain's label before its final TLD), not hand-restated. Adding a provider now means editing one set, and both the create-time reject path and the read-time risk-flag path pick it up automatically.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1`** \u2014 deleted its private `DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS` and now checks `DisposableEmailDomains.BLOCKLIST`; added the import. (`java.util.Set` import kept \u2014 still used by `PUBLIC_HOLIDAYS`.)\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 deleted its private `DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_BASE_LABELS` and now reads `DisposableEmailDomains.BASE_LABELS`; updated the Javadoc `{@link}`, added the util import, and removed the now-unused `java.util.Set` import.\n\nBehaviour is unchanged: the derived `BASE_LABELS` produces exactly `{mailinator, tempmail, guerrillamail}`, and the acceptance suite is green.",
